{0 AWS Core Guides}

The core package is a pure foundation plus explicit runtime adapters at the
edge. Service packages use this foundation for signing, request construction,
response metadata, and structured errors.

{1 Credentials}

Create explicit credentials with result-returning or exception-raising
constructors:

{[
let credentials =
  Awskit.Credentials.create_exn
    ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
    ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
    ()
]}

Temporary credentials include a session token:

{[
let credentials =
  Awskit.Credentials.create_exn
    ~access_key_id:"ASIATESTSESSIONTOKEN"
    ~secret_access_key:"secret"
    ~session_token:"FwoGZXIvYXdz..."
    ()
]}

Credentials are opaque. Use {!val:Awskit.Credentials.access_key_id} and
{!val:Awskit.Credentials.session_token} to read non-secret fields. The secret
access key is accepted at construction time but not exposed afterward.

{2 Environment Variables}

[awskit.unix] and [awskit.lwt_unix] can read:

{[
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
AWS_REGION
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
]}

The current credential support is intentionally small: static credentials and
environment variables. Shared AWS config files, named profiles, process
providers, web identity, ECS/EC2 metadata, STS assume-role, and refreshable
credentials are future work.

{1 Regions And Endpoints}

Regions are explicit values:

{[
let region = Awskit.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1"
]}

Endpoints are scheme/host/port records:

{[
let endpoint =
  Awskit.Endpoint.https
    ~host:"s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com"
    ()
]}

Runtime adapters can take endpoint overrides, but service-specific packages
should usually expose a provider model instead. For S3, prefer
[Awskit_s3.Provider] through [awskit-s3] adapters.

{1 Signing}

{!module:Awskit.Signing} implements AWS Signature Version 4 without IO.

Use [payload_hash] metadata instead of passing a raw body to signing:

{[
let signed =
  Awskit.Signing.sign_request
    ~credentials
    ~region
    ~service:"s3"
    ~method_:`PUT
    ~path:"/my-key"
    ~query:""
    ~headers:[ ("host", "my-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com") ]
    ~payload_hash:(Awskit.Body.Payload_hash.sha256_of_string "hello")
    ~now:Ptime.epoch
]}

For presigned or streaming cases, service packages may use explicit payload
hash values such as [UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD].

Useful low-level helpers:

{ul
  {li {!val:Awskit.Signing.uri_encode}}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Signing.canonical_query_params}}
  {li {!val:Awskit.Signing.ptime_to_date_time}}
}

{1 Structured Errors}

Core errors are structured values:

{[
type t =
  | Validation of validation
  | Signing of string
  | Transport of transport
  | Service of service
  | Decode of string
  | Body of body
]}

Use {!val:Awskit.Error.retry_class} for retry decisions:

{[
match Awskit.Error.retry_class err with
| `Throttled | `Retryable -> retry ()
| `Auth -> refresh_or_fail ()
| `Not_found -> handle_missing ()
| `Conflict | `Fatal | `Unknown -> fail err
]}

Service packages preserve {!type:Awskit.Error.t} and add classifier helpers.
For example, [awskit-s3] exposes [Awskit_s3.Error.is_no_such_key] and
[Awskit_s3.Error.is_precondition_failed].

{1 Runtime Body Model}

The runtime abstraction keeps HTTP metadata separate from bodies:

{ul
  {li upload bodies are passed separately to [Runtime.call]}
  {li download bodies are scoped through [Runtime.with_download_body]}
  {li request and response records are body-free}
}

This lets service packages stream bodies without forcing all adapters to buffer
data in memory.

Uploads carry a descriptor:

{[
{
  Awskit.Body.Upload.content_length = Some 5L;
  payload_hash = Awskit.Body.Payload_hash.sha256_of_string "hello";
  replayable = true;
}
]}

Downloads must be consumed inside [with_download_body]:

{[
Runtime.with_download_body body ~consume:(fun reader ->
  Runtime.read reader bytes ~off:0 ~len:(Bytes.length bytes))
]}

Service packages expose higher-level helpers when buffering is intentional.
For example, [awskit-s3] uses [Object.Buffer.get_string ~max_size].

{1 Choosing An Adapter}

{2 Eio}

Use [awskit.eio] for direct-style OCaml 5 applications:

{[
Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
let conn =
  Awskit_eio.create
    ~env
    ~sw
    ~region
    ~credentials
    ()
in
ignore conn
]}

The monad type is [type 'a t = 'a]. Errors are returned as [result] values by
service operations.

{2 Lwt Unix}

Use [awskit.lwt_unix] for ordinary Lwt applications on Unix:

{[
let conn =
  Awskit_lwt_unix.create
    ~region:"us-east-1"
    ~credentials
    ()
  |> Result.get_ok
]}

Operations in service packages return [Lwt.t].

{2 Generic Lwt}

Use [awskit.lwt] when you need to provide a custom [Cohttp_lwt.S.Client]:

{[
module Runtime = Awskit_lwt.Make (Cohttp_lwt_unix.Client)
]}

{1 Custom Runtime Adapters}

Implement {!module-type:Awskit.Runtime.S} when integrating another concurrency
or HTTP stack. The service package functors need:

{ul
  {li a monad type and bind/return}
  {li connection region, credentials, clock, and optional endpoint}
  {li upload-body constructors and upload descriptors}
  {li download-body scoped consumption}
  {li HTTP transport through [call]}
}

S3 adapters extend the core runtime with [s3_provider] so S3 endpoint
resolution can stay AWS-aware without putting S3 concepts into the pure core.
